> What version of ULTRIX.  Later versions (V4.3A and there-abouts)
> support scu(8), which allows access to the SCSI Reassign Block
> command with its "reassign lba" command.  On older versions you
> can use rzdisk with the -r option.

I have Ultrix4.4. I ran rzdisk -r block /dev/rrz* and it sounded 
like it failed, but the second attempt showed that the blocks were 
actually replaced. I also did the scanning for bad blocks with the 
same rzdisk utility before I tried to repair them (just to see if 
any more sprang up). And I am doing fsck on it right now. Thanks 
a lot!

> 
> If the driver had managed to read a good copy of the data on one
> of its many retries, it would have replaced the block for you
> and written the new block with the good data.  So, wherever the
> block is, it has corrupt data.  If fsck(8) is noticing the
> blocks then they're at least in the file system metadata space;
> cylinder group blocks, inodes, etc.  Fsck(8) doesn't read user
> data.
> 
> Once you fix those blocks, you'll need to run fsck again and
> see what's broken, note the files affected and use other tools
> to determine the full extent of the damage.  Some files may
> be lost, others may simply end up being misplaced (in lost+found).
> 
> You'll also want to scan for more bad blocks in the file data
> area.  Both scu(8) and rzdisk(8) have this feature.  If the
> disk isn't getting any better, then you may not be able to
> fix the blocks faster than new ones go bad.  If that's the
> case see if the conv=noerror option of dd(1) will let you
> skip over bad blocks.
> 
