> My customer has a MicroVAX 3400 running (probably; I can't
> confirm just now)  VMS V5.4-2, using TCP/IP by running one
> of the earliest releases of Wollongong TCP/IP software.
> This ran against the CPU card's built-in "thinwire" (coaxial)
> Ethernet port until a few months ago, then that "died" and he
> had to switch over to using the built-in "thickwire" Ethernet port
> and a thickwire-to-thinwire transceiver.  Now
> THAT has died, and he's called ME in.

Has someone verified that the ethernet segment does not have any problems
and somewhat conforms to the specifications?

> Neither panned out and all I could suggest was, "replace the
> CPU card" to get a whole new pair of built-in Ethernet ports.

Was the cabinet kit replaced also?

>    I just heard from him tonight that he did so -- and it did
> NOT fix the problem.

The first thing it to verify if the problem is with network hardware, the
VAX ethernet port, or the software running on VMS.

Create a small compliant thin-net network with only the VAX and a network
monitor on it.  That way you can easily confirm if the VAX is working.

With a network monitor working, you can try booting to the internal ethernet
card and watch the requests on the ethernet.

If that works, you know that the hardware is at least sending packets.

Then boot the VAX normally and use the TCP/IP program and the network
monitor using the PING command.  If that works, you know the problem is in
the other network hardware.

Having another system on the network that understands MOP, LAT, or another
protocol in common with the suspect system can help with the diagosis.
Network monitor programs are on the VMS freeware CD.

There are supposed to be downloadable network monitors for x86 PCs running
LINUX, or a commercial one available for Windows NT Server.

Troubleshooting networking hardware can be done cheaply with visual
inspection and isolating segments.  A time-domain reflectometer can be used
to quickly find physical defects in cables.

The things that can cause an ethernet segment to stop working reliably can
be endless.  An intermittent circuit that sometimes comes back to life (or
dies) when something is touched is a common problem in thin-net ethernet
circuits.  The TEE connectors can go bad this way, rare but it happens.

-John
