In article <gqqJ5.83590$JS3.12466496@typhoon.nyroc.rr.com>, "Chris Chiesa" <cchiesa1@rochester.rr.com> writes:
:   My customer has a MicroVAX 3400 running (probably; I can't confirm just
:now) VMS V5.4-2, using TCP/IP by running one of the earliest releases of
:Wollongong...

  AFAIK, Process now owns this TWG stuff.

:Neither panned out and all I could suggest was, "replace the CPU card" to
:get a whole new pair of built-in Ethernet ports.
:
:   I just heard from him tonight that he did so -- and it did NOT fix the
:problem.  I'm baffled.  I'm running out of "simple" hypotheses and am 
:starting to have to think of more exotic ones.

  I'd look at a network or network wiring problem -- when swapping the
  KA640 CPU, you swap both the bulkhead and the Ethernet controller.

:   1) He just so happened to replace his original CPU with another one
:broken in exactly the same manner.  Odds?  Seems unlikely.

  Yes, it does.  Or something else (host software or out on the 
  network somewhere) is causing the problem.

  Ping and the basic IP troubleshooting would be the first step,
  including a careful look at the network itself...  Swap some
  network cables around, check any bridge, router or delni, look
  for proper cable lengths and proper terminations, correct use
  of t-connectors (ThinWire) and such, etc.

:   If it's the software, we're still in a fix.  He probably doesn't have the
:original Wollongong installation tapes...I assume "current" TCP/IP packages 
:probably require newer VMS versions than V5.4-2, and that such "newer" VMS 
:versions are unsupported on hardware as ancient as a MicroVAX 3400-- 
:although they probably WILL RUN, bless 'em...

  OpenVMS VAX V7.2 and TCP/IP Services V5.0A is fully supported on this 
  hardware configuration.  The oldest currently-supported -- assuming
  a Prior Version Support contract is in place -- is V5.5-2 with some
  V4.* version of TCP/IP Services.

:...If we consider upgrading the VAX itself, we're still limited to Q-bus
:machines, because the machine is used as a turnkey system whose sole 
:purpose is to drive a properietary peripheral device whose driver 
:exists only in Q-bus form.
:
:    So, what are our options?...

  Some of the most obvious options:

    o Figure out how to replace the Q-bus widget.

      Implications include host software interface and whatever
      widgets exist on the backside of this Q-bus widget.

    o Upgrade to a faster MicroVAX or VAX 4000 series.  

      Implications include the potential for Q-bus widget and
      device driver problems due to the faster CPU, as this
      upgrade can reveal latent hardware and software problems.

    o Get it working again and leave it alone for five or ten years.


:    Please e-mail me even if you also reply here; my free time isn't what it
:used to be, and I'd do it for YOU if YOU were the one asking.

  Ask here, get an answer here.  My free time isn't what it used to be, too.

