Hello, all.


   Thanks to everyone who's responded thus far to my request for help on
the above-referenced subject.

   I'm going to combine my replies to all respondents, into one followup
post here, so that nobody gets just HALF of a big batch of individual re-
plies.

First, John E. Malmberg <wb8tyw@qsl.net> wrote in message
news:svckm9kuvsm293@corp.supernews.com...

> Has someone verified that the ethernet segment does not have any problems
> and somewhat conforms to the specifications?

If I remember my Ethernet topological jargon, this may be a non-issue as
there is only ONE "segment."  The entire network is a single linear run
of coaxial cables joined by T connectors.  No routers, gateways, etc. Is
this the same definition of "segment" you're using, John?  If so, I should
mention that there are twenty or so other workstations on this network /
segment, all of which continue to function and "inernetwork" perfectly on
the SAME backbone as the VAX-that-does-not.

For what it's worth, though, no, I don't think anybody's EVER been particu-
larly concerned with whether this segment "conforms to the specifications;"
I did my best, ten years ago, to whip up awareness of such things as mini-
mal-curving-radius, minimum/maximum cable lengths, and maximum segment
lengths, but not too many people paid much attention.  More on this below.

> Was the cabinet kit replaced also?

Yes.

> The first thing it to verify if the problem is with network hardware, the
> VAX ethernet port, or the software running on VMS.

Exactly what distinction are you making between "network hardware" and "the
VAX ethernet port?"  To me, the latter is PART OF the former.

I agree with you, but unfortunately I have no tools for this task at this
time.  Best we can do is observe the activity LED(s) on the transceiver(s)
as we try to Ping to/from the VAX.

> Create a small compliant thin-net network with only the VAX and a network
> monitor on it.  [...] There are are supposed to be downloadable network
> monitors for x86 PCs running LINUX, or a commercial one available for
> Windows NT Server.

(Forgive me for quoting some of your remarks out-of-sequence...)

Sounds like a great idea, if I can pull it off.  I don't have any Linux PCs
or NT Server machines at my own hands, and I doubt that my client does
either, but I'll definitely look into it.  Thanks for the pointers.

It's not entirely clear to me what you mean by a "compliant" network.  Just
properly-terminated, observing minimum-curving-radius and proper length on
all coax cables, etc.?  Or is there something more?  I must admit we were
always pretty lax on this, except on the rare occasions it arose to bite us
and we were FORCED to deal with it.

> With a network monitor working, you can try booting to the internal
ethernet
> card and watch the requests on the ethernet.

I assume by "booting to..." you mean what I'd call "booting from..." :-)  Is
it as simple as getting to the console prompt and issuing a 'b esa0' (or
whatever the Ethernet device name is)?  If not, please tell me the
procedure.
My VAX/VMS skills are embarrassingly rusty, and even in my prime I never
booted from Ethernet.

> Then boot the VAX normally and use the TCP/IP program and the network
> monitor using the PING command.  If that works, you know the problem is in
> the other network hardware.

What "other network hardware?"  Seems to me that if "that works," we've suc-
cessfully verified ALL network hardware having to do with the VAX.  Are you
saying some OTHER network hardware could be at fault here?

(Hmm. The VAX is at ONE END of a linear backbone.  If my client had recently
added a workstation to the network and exceeded the maximum cabling length,
I'd expect problems -- but could they be limited to "just the VAX" as we are
seeing?  Or would OTHER hosts have problems TOO?  Currently the 20+ other
hosts on the same backbone/segment are NOT having problems.)

> Having another system on the network that understands MOP, LAT, or another
> protocol in common with the suspect system can help with the diagosis.
> Network monitor programs are on the VMS freeware CD.

Alas, there are no other VMS systems available on this network.  I have lim-
ited access to a couple of other VAXes, but those with CD drives can't com-
municate in any way with those that might be made portable and brought to my
client's place.  This probably ISN'T going to happen.

> Troubleshooting networking hardware can be done cheaply with visual
> inspection and isolating segments.

If "segments" means "cables," we've already done this.  If "segments"
means "contiguous network topology elements," this is a non-issue as the
other hosts on this "same segment" are working fine.

> A time-domain reflectometer can be used to quickly find physical defects
> in cables.

We don't have a TDR.  I've only ever even SEEN one ONCE, and that many
years ago.  I believe it was rented for an exorbitant fee, and I have no
idea from where.  Suggestions?

> The things that can cause an ethernet segment to stop working reliably can
> be endless.  An intermittent circuit that sometimes comes back to life (or
> dies) when something is touched is a common problem in thin-net ethernet
> circuits.

Hmm.  That's interesting.  I was hoping our nine-plus years of successful
operation were a sign of stability.

> The TEE connectors can go bad this way, rare but it happens.

They've been swapped, several times among a handful of connectors, so I
think we've probably eliminated this problem.  I'll consider buying one or
two brand new ones, though, just to be safe.

