John,

You have run into one of the reasons why EISA proved unpopular over
the long haul.  The masterminds behind the EISA specification decided
that a configuration utility with CFG files was the way to go.  The
followed the example of IBM's late and lamentable MicroChannel.

The bottom line is this... 

If you have EISA cards, you need the EISA config files for them, and
you need to run the EISA config utility to make (most of) them usable
and useful.  If you have ISA or PCI cards, you don't need config files
for them, altho some ISA cards have config files to make them behave
better in EISA computers.  You can find the EISA config files for
whichever EISA cards you have on the card manufacturers' web sites,
provided they are still in business.  I once had the URL for a web
site that collected EISA config files, but I don't do a lot with EISA
lately, altho I have a stable of DEC servers with EISA slots, to be
used as spare parts or as replacements... Ben Myers

