In article <8spqrs01n51@news2.newsguy.com>, "Rich Beaudry" <r_beaudry@hotmail.com> writes:
:"Hoff Hoffman" <hoffman@xdelta.zko.dec.nospam> wrote in message
:news:8spnm8$csn$1@mailint03.im.hou.compaq.com...
:>   Nope.  If you do decide to mess around with the Q-bus, you will end
:>   up learning about CSRs and vectors and the serpentine grant continuity
:>   chain.  If you add or remove Q-bus cards, you can alter the settings
:>   that are required on one or more other Q-bus cards installed in the
:>   system.
:
:Interesting ... I've already gotten conflicting answers on this ... I'd
:rather not mess around with the internal cards, especially after reading
:more about the Q-bus... I'll try it without removing the cards...

  Removing a card at a fixed CSR outside of the floating addresses is easy.
  Adding a card with a fixed address is easy -- assuming there isn't another
  card sitting at the first address.  Knowing which cards are at fixed
  addresses isn't so easy -- it requires looking at the documentation for
  the particular module and/or some arcane knowledge of the Q-bus address 
  assignments (eg: CSR 760010 and up, vector 300 and up) and/or some amount
  of experimentation with the system and the configuration tools.  Adding 
  or removing a Q-bus card that is in the floating addresses (such as the 
  second and subsequent card where the first card goes at a fixed CSR and 
  vector -- this is a common case, and occurs when there are multiple Q-bus
  KDA50-like disk controllers to be installed and the first has a fixed
  address and all subsequent controllers are in the floating addresses) 
  can perturb zero or more other cards in the floating addresses, depending 
  on the ranking of the added or removed card.

  This doesn't even get into the discussion of the serpentine grant chain.

 --------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
