LAT does not "use" either MOP or DECnet. Try this, if you have a system
you can mess with: start up LAT and/or TCP/IP without starting either
DECnet or LANACP. It works very well.

As to other devices that can boot by MOP, how 'bout bridges, routers,
X.25 devices, SNA/Gateways not to mention LAVc satellite nodes which can
be so configured...

Starting to get the bigger picture now?

Perhaps you're being confused by a common misconception: that devices
which boot by MOP must be registered in a "boot node"'s DECnet database.
This is only true of devices which do not request a specific file in
their downline load request, or for which you want/need to provide a
file other than the one requested (I think). If a node can find the
requested file and has line service enabled on the circuit where the
load request was received, it will attempt to service the load request.

Don't believe me? Try it yourself.
 
[snip]
> DECnet and
> UCX do not have the change address attribute.  This means that UCX has to
> be started after DECnet but the others can be started at any time.

Try it once and see what happens.

If you get an error starting DECnet that says something about invalid
parameter value, that means DECnet must be started before the
protocol(s) you tried to start before DECnet.

(Specifically, DECnet-IV).

