helbig@astro.rug.nl writes...
}Right, fixed IP for VMS, but they need to be fixed and unique in the 
}world so that anyone (or just me; if I'm in an internet cafe oversees, I 
}am technically equivalent to anybody :-)) can get there.

Not necessarily.

The NAT scheme is the low-end solution for hanging multiple nodes off
the connection. The router has the "fixed and unique in the world"
address, and it can route incoming connections to precisely one
internal system per port number (as far as I know, I doubt that any
such devices do round-robin distribution or anything like that) - so
incoming the SMTP connections all go to one specific system, the
incoming port 80 HTTP connections all go to one specific system (you
can, of course, run web servers on other systems using other port 
numbers - one per system to allow connections to multiple servers),
the incoming TELNET connections (on the default port) all go to one
system, etc. Outgoing connections have the associations set up on
the fly, of course.

It has disadvantages, but for home use it is usually fine. Some of
the disadvantages can be worked around - you can run web servers
on different ports on different systems, set up telnet to accept
connections on different ports on different systems (preferably
in addition to the default one, rather than instead of it, to make
it easier for local use), etc. This may not be a great solution,
but it should work.

Of course, for telnet this is probably more complex than necessary.
You don't necessarily need multiple systems visible to the outside
world for telnet - just one. You connect to that one, then from there
you can connect to any of the others using the private addresses.

The other possibility is to forget NAT and get multiple IP addresses
(or an entire subnet) instead of a single IP address. This will be
more expensive, of course. 

--- Carl



