In article <39EE3EA9.99770822@earthlink.net>, "David J. Dachtera" <djesys.nospam@earthlink.net> writes:

  LAT and DECnet are totally unrelated.  LAT runs quite nicely with or 
  without DECnet, and DECnet runs quite nicely with or without LAT.

  Some LAT-based devices use do a DECnet-related protocol known as MOP for 
  downloads of the software that runs inside the particular widget -- this 
  same mechanism is used for OpenVMS Cluster Satellite downloads and for 
  network router downloads, for instance -- and you need to configure which 
  implementation of MOP should be used (eg: DECnet Phase IV, DECnet-Plus, 
  or LANCP) for the MOP download operations that will be performed by OpenVMS.

  There are LAT-based devices that do not need and do not use MOP at all.

  As for another part of this discussion: DECnet Phase IV likes to shuffle 
  the controller address around, and most (but not all) clients of the 
  network drivers can transparently tolerate the address shuffle, or they
  can be told to tolerate the address shuffle if it occurs, or they can be 
  told to default to the use of the Phase IV address and preempt the shuffle.
  One of the more common techniques for dealing with this involves starting 
  up DECnet first, of course.

 --------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
   Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




