NetBSD Bob wrote:
> This is what I was seeing, but I was thinking that perhaps there was
> a recommended secondary set of settings for the TK50, and if so, then
> that would be what I was after.  I understand it can float most anywhere,
> but it would be logical that they might suggest a particular point in
> floating space for it to reside.

I think you've misunderstood. For any given set of cards
there is one and only one acceptable set of settings. For most
configs your first TMSCP device will be at a fixed address
and your second will be at the secondary address. But that's
just because the rules work out that way. You get *no choice*
whatsoever in where the cards live. The rules don't suggest
where the card goes - they tell you firmly and unambigously.
It just so happens that the answer changes according to
exactly what you ask.

> Ohh, I would like to see that info, if you happen to lay hands thereupon.

There was a repost in here or comp.os.vms only a few days ago (within
the last week) of an old post that described the whole process. I didn't
read it in detail but it looked like ithad the necessary info.


> 
> That is what I have been hearing.  But, in my case, since the serial
> ports will only work a printer output, at best, can I move it down the
> line towards the end, and move other things up, to give them greater
> access to the cpu?  My big bottleneck is going to be disk I/O.  So,

If you think your serial card will slow down your disks then either
don't put it in the box or don't use it if it is there. I've never 
deliberately misconfigured a box this way - I imagine that the worst 
that will happen is that you will find your serial card drops
characters.

If the tape controller is forced to defer to your disk controller then
it will
at best be terribly slow and at worst will fail to write properly. Given
that 
the most likely source or sink of I/O for your tape is the disk
controller,
this will probably be very bad. Configure it the right way and you will
have 
a happy, contented system.


> I would have to kludge up an extender panel of some sort for the
> cpu OCP(?) or see if there was some other cabkit that handled that.
> If it is a total no-go, I have a lead on a BA215 cabinet that I am
> working on, and maybe..... more fun....

At worst you would have to hang the proper H3602(?) BA200-series
console faceplate out of the side of your BA123. Ugly. But it may
well work.

