In article <8snla802oj2@news1.newsguy.com>, r_beaudry@hotmail.com says...

> I hooked up a terminal to the serial console port (upper right hand corner
> of the plate where all the connections are made -- There is also a LED
> display, a baud rate switch, and a couple of other switches right next to
> this plate).  By powering up, and pressing the "Halt" button on the front
> repeatedly, I did get a ">>>" prompt.
> 
> Unfortunately, I am stuck at this point...

	You will be unless you remove the graphics hardware, and rearrange 
the internal boards to maintain bus grant continuity. The VCB02 graphics 
option, which is what turns a MicroVAX II into a VAXStation II, takes 
over as the console if it is installed.

> Here is what I want to do:
> 
> - Be able to boot the system, and log in and use the system via a serial
> port.  An RGB monitor is financially out of the question for me, and a
> serial port is not all that bad.

	Ok. That being the case, your best bet is to remove the VCB02 kit 
(consists of three boards) and do some rearranging of the remaining 
boards so you don't end up with a bus error.

	The VCB02 kit consists of two or three boards: One M7168 and one or 
two M7169's. The 7168 is the graphics controller, while the 7169's 
provide four color planes each.

	WARNING! Placing the wrong board in the wrong slot can mean 
trouble. If you've never worked on VAXen hardware before, I would 
strongly suggest you look at the VAX hardware FAQ at:

	http://anacin.nsc.vcu.edu/~jim/mvax/mvax_faq.html

	Another good one is:

	http://www.vaxarchive.org/hw/

	Once you get settled with the hardware, I would suggest NetBSD as 
an OS. It's a free Unix-type system, the information on which may be 
found at: http://www.netbsd.org

> The system has 4 hard drives, and I was told they are each 650MB.  It also
> has two CD Readers (RRD50, I think?), and a tape drive (tk50?)

	Four hard drives? Hmmmm! That could mean one of three things.

	1). A maxed-out RQDX3 controller (four 170MB MFM drives).

	2). A third-party ESDI controller and four ESDI drives.

	3). A third-party or DEC SCSI host adapter and four SCSI drives (if 
you're REALLY lucky... QBus SCSI adapters are rare and expensive!)

	Two CD's doesn't surprise me. Yes, probably RRD50's or the older 
42's.

> - Can I just let the system eventually boot up on its own, and just hang a
> serial terminal onto it to login and use it?  If I need to boot from the
> console, what's the command to do so?

	Not until you remove the graphics glop. As for booting, there are a 
number of different commands. The correct one depends on what device you 
want to boot from. Unlike a PC, a MicroVAX can boot from just about any 
peripheral on the bus (assuming, of course, that there's bootable media 
in said peripheral).

> - I read somewhere that I may need to physically remove the graphics cards
> in order to use it the way I want to.  Is that true, and can anyone tell me
> what cards they are, and what traps to look out for?

	See above. Read the FAQs. Wear an anti-static wrist strap. Be 
careful.

> - Are the serial ports on the back (there are 8) wired the same as a PC
> serial port?  If not, how can I make up the cable so I can plug it into my
> PC (which is running a VT100 emulator)?

	No, the pinout is different. Check the FAQ for details.

	Good hunting!

