"Shane J. Wolfe" wrote:
> 
> Hi all!
> I have a DEC 3000 that is booting into single user mode all the time.  The
> file system is mounting read only. 

In single user mode, you will be running a Bourne shell.  The command

# bcheckrc

_should_ fsck and mount all filesystems declared in /etc/fstab.  If
there are
no complaints from fsck, then exiting single user mode will bring the
system
up in multiuser mode (starting the X Window System and login prompter).

I suspect that the OSFLAGS variable is set to "s" in the SRM console
(>>> prompt).
The next time you are in SRM console ( CTRL/P during auto-reboot, or
issue
# shutdown -h now
in single user mode), do a 

>>> show

to see the variable names & values.  I think 'OSFLAGS' is the right
name; if it
is, and the value is 'S', do a 

>>> set OSFLAGS A

to have it autoboot to multiuser mode.  The command

>>> boot -fl "a"

will have the same effect, one time only.

>                                     The Windowing system is also locking up
> after the system boots.  I'll type exit to bring up the grphical login under
> the windowing system.  It logs in just fine, but then stops right after the
> login, like it's locked, but the mouse still works. It's running Digital
> Unix v3.0.  Does the fact that it is mounting read only have anything to do
> with the windowing system locking up?   

In a word, "Yes."  X Window System needs to write in /tmp and/or
/usr/tmp (sorry,
don't have a system handy to check which).

