In article <8snla802oj2@news1.newsguy.com>, "Rich Beaudry" <r_beaudry@hotmail.com> writes:
:First of all, let me say I am very experienced with PCs, and the PC
:Architecture...

  That could well be more of a liability here than a use, as it will 
  tend to lead you to certain assumptions on the way things work.

:The wife of a friend here worked for DEC, and got her hands on a
:VAXStation-II/GPX just before they folded.

  When DIGITAL was bought by Compaq, you mean.  My desk hasn't moved. :-)

  As a VAXstation II/GPX is roughly fifteen year old VAX hardware -- 
  really old, really slow, and rather more difficult to work with than 
  any more recent VAX and Alpha hardware, and not particularly SCSI-capable.

  And as this is a Q-bus system, it will be rather more difficult for an
  inexperienced user -- no offense intended -- to configure and run.
  Please search the OpenVMS Ask The Wizard area for "serpentine" and
  "Q-bus" for some background.  See www.openvms.compaq.com/wizard

  This would NOT be my first choice as a hobbyist system -- well, not
  for an inexperienced user.  Given the relative prices of new (and far 
  faster) entry-level hardware, I'd look at spending any of the "play" 
  money that might become available for a hobby on a more recent (and
  pre-owned) AlphaStation series box, rather than on new hardware for 
  use on a VAXstation II series.

:...The only problem with it is that the RGB monitor has died...

  The OpenVMS FAQ has some details.  www.openvms.compaq.com

:I hooked up a terminal to the serial console port (upper right hand corner
:of the plate where all the connections are made -- There is also a LED
:display, a baud rate switch, and a couple of other switches right next to
:this plate).  By powering up, and pressing the "Halt" button on the front
:repeatedly, I did get a ">>>" prompt.

  That's a good sign.  You can also use a BREAK on the console serial
  line.  See the OpenVMS FAQ for details on the MicroVAX console.

:Unfortunately, I am stuck at this point...

  Usually little more than "BOOT" is required.

  Directions on breaking into the system -- since you probably do not
  know the SYSTEM password -- are included in the OpenVMS FAQ.

:- Be able to boot the system, and log in and use the system via a serial
:port.

  Works fine.  Fully supported.

:The system has 4 hard drives, and I was told they are each 650MB.

  If these are 650 MB disks, they would likely be third-party disks.
  The usual disks within the enclosures -- BA23, BA123, or rack-mount
  BA23 -- are RD54 159MB disks.  The bigger disks -- typically only
  in the rack-mount -- are RA-series disks, and these require a KDA50
  series controller.

:It also has two CD Readers (RRD50, I think?), and a tape drive (tk50?)

  Those look to be expected hardware -- RRD50 is a first-generation CD-ROM.
:
:Here are my questions:
:
:- Can I just let the system eventually boot up on its own, and just hang a
:serial terminal onto it to login and use it?  

  Yes, you can.

:If I need to boot from the console, what's the command to do so?

  BOOT, typically.  Please see the OpenVMS FAQ for the "I forgot the 
  system password" boot sequence.

:- I read somewhere that I may need to physically remove the graphics cards
:in order to use it the way I want to.  Is that true, and can anyone tell me
:what cards they are, and what traps to look out for?

  Nope.  If you do decide to mess around with the Q-bus, you will end
  up learning about CSRs and vectors and the serpentine grant continuity 
  chain.  If you add or remove Q-bus cards, you can alter the settings 
  that are required on one or more other Q-bus cards installed in the
  system.

:- Are the serial ports on the back (there are 8) wired the same as a PC
:serial port?

  Please see the OpenVMS FAQ.

:If not, how can I make up the cable so I can plug it into my
:PC (which is running a VT100 emulator)?

  Please see the OpenVMS FAQ.

:I can give more details on the system if needed...

  You will want to provide the system model number -- I will assume that
  the enclosure has not been changed from what was shipped -- and you
  will want to provide the "M" numbers off the spines of the Q-bus cards
  in the Q-bus backplane.

