Steve,

The Wintel Multias are all made for Pentium CPUs operating at 3.3v.
Your best bet is either an Intel Pentium OverDrive @ 200Mhz or an
Intel Pentium MMX @ 233Mhz, the latter with a PowerLeap or similar
adapter.

I don't think you'll ever find a BIOS upgrade for it.  If either of
the above CPUs work (probable), you can bet that the BIOS will not
report the CPU speed correctly.  In 99.9% of the motherboards I've
encountered, failure to report CPU speed correctly is entirely
cosmetic, and the CPU still runs at rated speed as measured by one of
the several public domain CPU speed calibration programs.  Of course,
motherboard jumpers/switches MUST be set for a 66Mhz external bus
clock.

I don't give much hope to a Multia working with a non-Intel CPU (AMD
or Cyrix-Yuck!), but you never know... Ben Myers

