> Again, why not?  You certainly can run a web server.  Finding it is the
> problem.  The dynamic DNS provider can also simply this.

For web serving, the LINUX folks have written a HOW-TO on this.  They use a
script to detect when the DHCP address changes, and then they send an FTP
update to the static front page that the ISP provides, that changes it's
links to redirect them to the new IP address.

You can even use a web page to redirect file uploads and downloads to your
home machine.

There are even programs available for Windoze to do this.

E-mail of course requires a DNS that can accept the updates, but web serving
does not.

Also with DHCP, you usually keep the same address unless you disconnect from
the network for longer then the lease time, or the ISP shuts down the DHCP
server for the same length of time.

