Phillip Helbig wrote:
> Will this allow IP access on demand from outside and inside and allow a
> domain name to be associated with my stuff?  Is NAT compatible with VMS?

ISDN may not allow "on demand" line setup for inbound connections since your
ISP won't see you while not connected and won't know who to call since you
don't "own" the IP address.

With a fixed IP address, the ISP will have an equivalence between IP address
and phone number, so when an inbound connection comes, the ISP is able to dial
your ISDN modem to establish the link. But with a dynamic address, they won't
do that.

With ADSL and DSL (and cable), these are permanent always-on connections, so
inbound calls are technically possible. The trick is to have the users know
what IP address is assigned to you at this moment.

Another important point is to read the fine print on your ISP's contract. Even
if technically, you might be abe to find ways to allow inbound connections,
doing so may violate your subscription.

As far as NAT is concerned, your will require this for VMS. Since VMS does not
have a DHCP client (yet), it needs to be given a fixed IP address. By buying a
router that does NAT, it allows you to run your local TCP network with local
(10.* for instance) fixed adresses. The router does the DHCP stuff with the
ISP without the VAXes being aware since they always talk to the same box (the
router) with the same IP on your side.

And you can program your router to route incoming calls to a specific machine.
(for instance, inbound calls to port 80 (http) may go to VAX1, while incoming
calls to port 25 (SMTP) might be routed to your VAX2 on your lan.



