I think that the rules for configuring Qbus CSRs mean that a few
common cards always have fixed addresses and the rest float. So
the first TMSCP device (which is what TQK50 and TQK70 cards are)
will (almost?) always live at the same address; similarly
the primary MSCP device (e.g. RQDX3) and primary ethernet (DELQA)
will also live at the same address ... none of which conflict
with each other. Less common stuff tends to float depending on
what else is in there.

At least one of the manuals I have describes the mechanical process
you go through of working out where stuff lives given a list
of what you have. If only I could remember which of the manuals
actually describes this :-)

But it doesn't really matter since SYSGEN on VMS (and presumably the
posted SYSGEN code) and CONFIG on the later consoles do all the
thinking for you. So I've never had to work it out by hand.

> True, but, if it makes a 30-50% difference in polling or interrupts
> or whatever stealing CPU cycles, then on an MVII, that is enough
> to warrant tuning the thing a little.

I doubt that you'd get that much of a speed up unless you
seriously misconfigured stuff.
 
> Which micronote, offhand, or is there a way to search for it in
> particular, or do I need to download the whole kit and kaboodle
> and excercise my grep?

Amongst other places, they live here:

http://www.ibiblio.org/pub/academic/computer-science/history/pdp-11/hardware/micronotes/numerical/

Start with #41 (and then read the rest!)

Essentially if you had an RQDX3 instead of your SCSI device you
would normally have an ethernet controller after the CPU and memory,
then the tape and lastly the bus controller. This would be a
"conventional" configuration. 

Typically serial controllers don't like to be kept waiting for
a bus access (when they need it, they really need it otherwise
they will drop a character ... and they don't chew up much bus time
themselves) so a DHV11 would go even nearer the CPU than an
ethernet controller.

I've never used a board which uses interrupt level 5 (like
yours seems to) so you'll have to take into account other
comments that suggest you must have your SCSI controller as
the next thing after CPU/memory (or at least electrically before
anything that uses the interrupt/dma request lines).
 
