They're entirely different protocols.  MOP is as well, but is built into
DECnet.

>> other than on older versions of VMS

>You mean DECnet/VMS...

No, I meant VMS.  LANCP comes with VMS, and no changes are required to
DECnet to use LANCP, other than configuring DECnet not to try and start
up MOP.

>> (before LANCP) you need DECnet to MOP boot any

>...thing that needs a downline load and only supports MOP for this, such
>as certain ...

>> terminal servers that need
>> it.

We were talking about LAT here.  I know of no other uses of MOP/DECnet
by LAT other than the mentioned terminal servers.

>> >If you intend to use DECnet at all, you must start it before any other
>> >networking software. Other than that, there is no dependency.
>> 
>> Only necessary for network user services that don't have the "can change
>> address" attribute set.  LAT does. 

>Does it? I'd double-check that, if I were you.

I checked the protocols on the system at work and it does.

>> SCS (clustering) does

>...not always use the same interface (NI) primarily (mostly uses the CI
>(if available) or the MC (memory channel, if available). LAVc and SCSI
>clustering uses primarily the NI which, of course, begins long before
>any thing else gets fired up.

NI-SCS always starts itself on all LAN adapters it finds, even if not
necessary.  I'm not talking whether or not it's necessary or used.

>As my 5th grade teacher said, "Be specific, cite examples."

I just gave two.

I'll check again the system at work tomorrow, but ones I remember that do
are LAT, MOP, the SYSID/remote console protocol (the last 2 are by LANCP),
LAST, NI-SCS, and a couple others I don't remember offhand.  DECnet and
UCX do not have the change address attribute.  This means that UCX has to
be started after DECnet but the others can be started at any time.

