You will need to confirm what the Dilogic SCSI card is doing on the bus.

Here there be dragons!!!

It could be emulating one or more standard Digital Disk Controller(s).

It could be emulating one or more standard Digital Tape Controller(s).

It could be emulating both at once.

It could have NVRAM or jumpers (likely NVRAM) that configure how it looks on
the bus.

It could be requiring a Dilogic SCSI driver for your target operating
system.

There were also a number of 8 port serial cards.  To do a configuration, the
precise model number may be important.

> Hoff Hoffman <hoffman@xdelta.zko.dec.nospam> wrote:
> >   Seriously, a Q-bus system is not a good hobbyist system
> >   -- well, not for folks inexperienced with maintaining the Q-bus.
>
> Nah, it just requires some ferreting out mystical DECisms de
> computerese.  As usual, manuals de info went one way and
> the machine another.  I get to ferret out the pieces to make
> it plug and play.  Why do all the hardware
> manuals get non-existent?  grrrrr!

That is part of the fun of learning the Q-bus and restoring Antique
Computers.

You might try searching for DILOG on a search engine to see if you can get
some technical information on your board.

You may need to boot a diagnostic program to interrogate it's current
configuration.

> Come on, folks, this is a dumb MVII, has has only three
> ordinary addin cards, nothing special.

The SCSI card is not ordinary.  A good find, and possibly more valuable then
the rest of the machine if it can handle a CD-ROM, and looks like a Digital
MSCP disk controller.

> The 8 port serial is first, the scsi second, and the TK70
> third.  Why should it be that hard to address up a TK50
> as a secondary tape drive?

As of now, from the information you have given, we do not know if you
already have multiple tape drives configured on your box.

You may need to configure the TK50 as a third drive (if it allows this)

Based on my past experiences with third party disk/tape controllers, if you
were to boot VMS on the system and stress the 8 port serial card and the
disk/tape card at the same time, it will probably crash.

The third party disk and tape controllers were usually set for BIRQ5 not
BIRQ4 like most of the Digital branded board.  As such, it must be closer to
the CPU physically on the bus than the serial card.  This also needs to be
verified by you.

> It lines up something like:
>
>  SCSI HD CARD   8 PORT SERIAL  ARBITRATOR   ARBITRATOR   4M RAM   KA630
CPU
>                                            (for missing
>                                              ram card)
>  TK70 CARD
>
> When I can find another ram card, I will add it in, but as I understand
it,
> ram cards don't come into play, here.

Correct.

> When I can find a DELQA, I will add that in.  I understand it goes on the
> end of the line, and might have a fixed addressing, so it should also not
> be a problem, here.

That is probably true for the first DELQA.  I can not say that with absolute
certanty though.

I generally see them closer to the CPU than the serial cards.

> For the sake of discussion, where would the TK50 card best go, and
> what jumpers/switches to set as a secondary tape system?  The TK70
> I want to retain as the primary tape, since it read and functions
> much better than a TK50.

Until you find out exactly what the configuration of the Dilog card is, all
is unknown.

> I don't have the M numbers off the cards, but they are standard issue
> DEC, except for the Dilogic scsi card.  The 8 port serial is an M31xx
> something from memory, and the TK70 card is stock.  IF the M numbers
> are that important, I can open the machine up when I get home and
> write them down.  I gotta add in the third drive, tonight, anyway.

The module numbers or common names for the cards are important.

The information on the Dilogic SCSI card is critical!

With out knowing how that card is currently addressed, it is impossible to
predict the settings for the rest of the Q-Bus.


> That's it!
>
> >   Emphasis on ALL Q-bus devices.  I repeat: ALL Q-bus devices.  I say
> >   again: ALL -- do NOT omit any Q-bus devices from the Q-bus
configuration
> >   process, or the resulting Q-bus configuration settings can end up
> >   non-functional.  If you don't know what the Q-bus widget is, then
> >   (assuming it is a DIGITAL Q-bus board) post the M number from the
> >   spine of the card and somebody here can look it up for you.
>
> ALL, ALL, ALL..... given above.....
>
> Thanks, Steve, and all, and other than being rather short-ended without
> an ethernet card, it is not that bad a little machine, once two or three
> large HDs play in it.  Ram is the main gotcha.  The main use for the TK50
> as a secondary is to write BSD boot tapes for it.  If I could get say
> a TKZ10 to run on it, that would be the best world.  As I understand it,
> that could require an additional controller for that if the scsi
controller
> won't handle the tape (some did and some didn't I am told).  If not, I am
> stuck with the TK50 for writing boot tapes, and incoming tarballs.

The TK70 tape can read TK50 tarballs.  No problem.  The TK70 can write boot
tapes that a TK70 can boot from.  No problem.

The TK70 can not write a TK50 tape for another system to use.  An
unfortunate limitation of it's time.

Used TK50Z (external SCSI) versions are available.  There are two versions.
One made for connection only to VAX/VAXstation 2000s, and others that are
alleged to be more SCSI compliant.

I suspect that Q-BUS scsi controllers that emulate a bootable Digital widget
will sell for far more than what it would cost for you to get a faster
machine, but again, I am not current on prices.

I think it already has been noted that not all q-bus memory boards for VAXen
go with all CPU cards.

