> That is highly likely.  It is not the high-end card, though, the SQ739.

If we assume your SCSI card pretends to be an MSCP disk controller
(i.e. you kick start the box with B DUA0 or some such) and that you 
have a DHV11 and a TK50 and a TK70 in the box you need:

Device: UDA     Name: PUA        CSR: 772150     Vector: 154    Support:
yes
Device: TU81    Name: PTA        CSR: 774500     Vector: 260    Support:
yes
Device: TU81    Name: PTB        CSR: 760404*    Vector: 300*   Support:
yes
Device: DHV11   Name: TXA        CSR: 760440*    Vector: 310*   Support:
yes


The UDA is your MSCP-SCSI translating card (it pretty much has to do
this
if you expect to boot your MicroVAX), either of the TU81 lines
can be your TK50 and the other can be your TK70.

I have a DHV11 manual if you need to change its settings.

> Well, If it does, I can yank the 8 port serial card, since I do most
> everything off the console port.  The only use for a serial card is
> maybe to run a printer port.

If you drop the serial card none of the other settings change (lucky
you!).

> > The third party disk and tape controllers were usually set for BIRQ5 not
> > BIRQ4 like most of the Digital branded board.  As such, it must be closer to
> > the CPU physically on the bus than the serial card.  This also needs to be
> > verified by you.
> 
> The serial is first and the HD scsi card is second in the line, just
> as illustrated below.
> 
> >> It lines up something like:
> >>
> >>  SCSI HD CARD   8 PORT SERIAL  ARBITRATOR   ARBITRATOR   4M RAM   KA630
> >>                                            (for missing
> >>                                              ram card)
> >>  TK70 CARD

You have the SCSI card last (remember that in the BA123 the Q-Q
grant pattern goes down slot 5 - where you have a DHV11 - 
and up slot 6 - where you have a TQK70 and a SCSI card).

I don't know whether your SCSI card needs to be nearer the CPU than
the other cards - but if it does, it's the furthest!

> OK, the thing has:
> 
> (*)       SQ706A     M3104     M9047     M9047     M7608     M7606
>           HD SCSI    DHV11A    grant     grant     MS630     KA630
>                                          (***)
> (**)      M7559
>           TKQ70
> 
> I probably want the TKQ50 at (*), a DELUA at (**), and a 4/8 Meg
> ram card at (***).
> 

The second RAM card can go at *** but as you say it has no bearing
on anything else (except that you probably would prefer to
run with 16MB if at all possible).

Generally the last thing (electrically) on the bus is the disk
controller
and before that you have the tape controllers. One of the Micronotes
that
Tim Shoppa put on the web discusses general guidlines.

