> I don't know whether your SCSI card needs to be nearer the CPU than
> the other cards - but if it does, it's the furthest!

If the DILOG SCSI card is BIRQ 5, it must be moved closest to the CPU!

This is a known issue with the CSC, and is documented in detail in the
VMS 5.x guide for writing device drivers.

This is true on all VMS Q-bus systems.

Since it is a hardware issue, it should be relevant to the *NIXes
that can run on VAX hardware also.

>  
> Generally the last thing (electrically) on the bus is the
> disk controller and before that you have the tape controllers.
> One of the Micronotes that Tim Shoppa put on the web discusses
> general guidelines.

Some of the original documentation is wrong on the card placement for
BIRQ5 devices.  Beware!

Again, any bus cards that are strapped higher than BIRQ4 must be
closer to the CPU than any serial cards.

Most Digital branded cards are BIRQ4.  There are some exceptions
though.  Notably a device known as a DTQNA produced by the former
Digital Networking group.


Most of the third party disk and tape controller cards that I have
encountered are at BIRQ5.  A few of the later models could be
adjusted to BIRQ4 because of the problems if they are put at the
end of a Q-bus.

That the BIRQ5 devices must be closer to the CPU than the BIRQ4
ones is documented in the current Version of the OpenVMS manuals
about writing device drivers.

The VMS V5 manuals seemed to be more specific about BIRQ4 serial
boards causing crashes when closer to the CPU than BIRQ5 boards.

And from personal experience, it does not take much effort to crash
an OpenVMS system when this rule is violated.

-John
wb8tyw@qsl.network



