> unfortunately the machine that this was connected to (3100) is being
> retired... but it is a great monitor. any help appreciated!

If the monitor supports the new "Plug & Play" standard, a monitor driver can
tell the operating system the monitor size and some other characteristics,
and if it can support an "Energy Star" standby power mode.

This of course requires a video card that can receive that information from
the monitor over the video lines.

I have not seen the documentation for that protocol, or have a list of what
video cards can support it.

For monitors and video card combinations that do not support this standard,
the monitor driver for Windows is simply an information file that has this
information in a table for that specific monitor.  It is considered easier
for a consumer to enter in a monitor name, and possibly load a "driver"
disk, then to give then a chart to enter in the size, resolution, and
synchronization freqencies in a chart.

Of course, not all monitor vendors have driver disks, and if by chance some
of the tables for the monitor characteristics are wrong, there is no
documented way to fix them.  You would need to get the monitor vendor to
issue a patch.

(The Windows NT version of QuickRES seems to know how to fix them on Windows
95, but the Windows 95 version of QuickRes does not.)

Windows NT 3.x and 4.0 do not use monitor drivers, so you have to set the
synchronization frequency manually.

For LINUX, I think that one of the X-11 configuration utilities will allow
you to enter in the data.

I do not know the specifics on the VRC20-Ha, and if it can be interrogated
by a video card.  I also do not know if there are any drivers for LINUX
video cards that support interrogating the monitor.

