On Friday the Chapter settled an outstanding legal case and recovered the
money owed us from our previous Tradeshow manager.  This case had been going
on for over 2 years.  What happen was they refused to release the money owed
us being held in the receipts account.  Of course they had a series of phony
reasons why not to release it.

We have had to be very quiet about the other legal case involving the loss
on
Philly.  We were owed 600K and will never collect a dime.  What happened for
the first time ever to my knowledge when DECUS has had to take legal action
is
the other party challenged DECUS's legal status. The case was thrown out of
court within 6 weeks of it being filed.  The reason why was we are a
incorporated association.  As such it is very hard for the members to be
sued
but also it is impossible for us to sue.  The membership itself can
collectively sue but they can sue for their personal damages i.e. if we lost
600K but you the member couldn't demonstrate how it personally cost you
money
there is no legal course of action to remedy this.

Obvious if the Tradeshow company had heard about this they could have done
the
exact same thing and had the case tossed out of court and it would have cost
us another 300K - hence the reason why we have been very quiet about what
happened.

The bottom line is there is virtually no means that DECUS can use to protect
itself against these types of problems as a incorporated association.  We
were
basically lucky this didn't come back and bite us for the first 37 years
DECUS
existed.

This has now been looked at by 3 teams of lawyers and they all came to the
same conclusion.  DECUS cannot execute contracts counting on the courts to
be
there to protect the rights and assets of the Chapter...



