The Board approved the whole package to my knowledge - I was not in the room
at the time.

Mr. Erbland in several places you have suggested that "the membership" did
not approve this change.  I would like to remind all that 63% of the voting
membership did approve this change.  A shift of 48 votes would have produced
2/3's vote required in the first bylaw vote.  Additionally amendments to
address the issues raised in the original CON statement were added.  Please
stop posting notes that suggest this action was taken again the will of "the
membership".  The most you can claim Mr. Erbland is that it was contrary to
the will of a minority of the membership.

The bylaws may or may not be agreed to by the membership by lets have a
honest public discussion.  Misrepresenting the facts, even by errors of
omission, should not occur.  We are a technical professional society and not
a dirty tricks political debating club.  Nor should anyone be using this
bylaw vote to do something so self-serving as to use it as a springboard to
attempt to gain election to the Board.  That is risking this User Groups
future for personal political gain. The facts are as follows...

1) The issues raised in the original CON statement were LONG STANDING issues
and NOT a whole NEW set of issues triggered by changes in these bylaws.  The
only significant intended changes in these bylaws is to allow Encompass to
incorporate and to switch the director terms from 2 to 3 years.  This User
Group has recently found out, to the tune of $600,000, that as a
unincorporated association it is next to impossible to enforce our legal
rights.

2) A bylaw committee was form to address the LONG STANDING issues raised in
the CON statement and amendment language is on the ballot for the members to
consider.

3) The staff support this User Group has enjoyed is gone.  While Compaq is
more than willing to spend at least the same dollars supporting this User
Group they are no long willing to do it via staff FTE's.  As of 1/1/2001 we
will have no staff support.  CETS2000 occurred because of the extraordinary
efforts of some folks that will not be repeated.  Future current services
like CETS2001 likely will not happen with much User Group involvement if
these bylaws are rejected.  Additionally there is a whole bunch of new stuff
like developing LUGs and SIGs again that can't happen until we have the
staff in place to support them.

4) Until we are a legal corporation we cannot enter into long term binding
contracts that we are sure the courts will enforce.  Until we can enter into
contracts we cannot obtain the staff support to run this organization.
Additionally we want this to form as a not-for-profit corporation so we
won't be liable for taxes.

5) Please ask anyone who suggests there are far reaching changes beyond
incorporation being introduced by these bylaws to document them.  There are
no such changes being introduced.

I have no debate with those who say the Board could have handled much this
better.  I am not a member of the current Board (and never will be a member
of the Board again) but I know I would have handled this differently.
However, I see little point in causing serious damage to the ability of this
User Group to deliver and expand services in order to work this issue with
the Board.  One could easily aregue most of the issues result from not
having a Executive Director who should be handling these details and not the
Board anyway.  Again until we gain legal status we cannot enter into the
long term contracts required to obtain an Executive Director.  You will
often see folks say "the Board" failed to do something.  The fact is that
for the most part in a normal association these levels of details would not
be seen as a Board operational responsibility but the responsibility of a
Executive Director.

This is too important an issue to demagogue in the name of attempting to run
for the Board of Directors of this User Group...



