must, itself, be a legally empowered/recognizable entity.

What's in a name? That's up to you. If voting for the by-laws will
create a "legally empowered/recognizable entity", does it matter whether
they call it "DECUS", "EnCompAss", or anything else?

IMO...

"DECUS" is now more a paradigm of how the symposia should be shaped and
conducted than the name of a user society. "D.E.C." no longer exists,
except in our memories. So, the name itself has certain associations,
but not much meaning beyond that. The fact that I personally cling to it
is today more a statement of personal convictions that have yet to be
reconciled with Compaq than anything else.

Even "Enterprise Computing Association" falls somewhat short, but is
nonetheless the name chosen to replace "DECUS". I don't like it, but
there is little I can do about it other than state my protests. The term
"Enterprise", while not of itself exclusive since it (by dictionary
definition) includes businesses of all sizes, has acquired a meaning
that refers only to "big business" - an exclusive connotation.

Still, the user society need to achieve a certain, legally
recognized/recognizable status. That entity needs a name.

That which we call a rose, by another name, would smell as sweet. I
think that's how it goes - I'm a not student of the "Bard" (don't go
there, please).

