"Robert Alan Byer" <byer@mail.ourservers.network> wrote
in message news:UMEG4e5b5KFT@cartman.ourservers.net...
>
> Question...
>
> I'm porting over a Unix application to OpenVMS Alpha and all is
> fine and well with one exception.
>
> One of the modules makes use of..
>
> #pragma auto_inline off
>
> and I get a compile message about the pragma "auto_inline" is
unrecognized.
> The program compiles and runs just fine as far as I can tell.
>
> (I'm new to pragma so I haven't a clue as to what it's supposed to be
doing.)
>
> My question is, is their an OpenVMS equiv. I can use or can I just comment
it
> out and take my chances.

The #pragma is a preprocessor directive that is compiler specific.

http://www.openvms.digital.com/commercial/c/5492p019.htm#pragmas

You need to check the compiler manual for the specific compiler that the
program was originally compiled on to verify what the #pragma setting does.

I would suspect that it is telling the compiler not to replace functions in
a module with inline code.  Basically a deoptimization for speed in some
cases.

As to if you can ignore that, it depends on the reason for that directive.

You can follow the above link for the DEC C #pragma values.

As you probably can guess, a #pragma should not be used in a portable
program unless preprocessor directives also test for the compiler name.

-John
wb8tyw@qsl.network







