In article <39eb1a49$1@news.kapsch.co.at>, eplan@kapsch.net (Peter LANGSTOEGER) writes:
:In article <39eb162b.10420253@news.ggn.net>, vincent.saladino@gsxxi.com (Vinnie Saladino) writes:
:>I have an Alpha running OpenVMS V 7.1-2.  If I reach the Maxusers
:>limit on the License, isn't the "SYSTEM" account still able to log in
:>to administer the machine ?
:
:No. The OPA0: device is.

  There is a documented back-door via OPA0: for licensing- or authorization-
  related failures, but this situation is not particularly a failure.

  When you reach your licensed number of users, then you have reached the 
  maximum number of users.  I am aware of no back-door (via the console or
  otherwise) to add one more user, you need to have one or more of these 
  users log out or you need to acquire additional user licenses.  You can
  nuke a user via remote SYSMAN or AMDS or (if in a cluster) via a STOP/ID
  and the cluster-wide process services.

  Folks might be incorrectly remembering the extra login that was available
  for system management purposes (and not interactive use) in this context, 
  and that extra login was removed some time ago based on the availability 
  of various remote system management tools.

 --------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
