BSD Bob the old greybeard BSD freak wrote:
> 
> I was looking on the label of an RF35 drive and it says ``RF35/RZ35''
> on it.  I was thinking RF was DSSI and RZ was plain SCSI?  What gives?

Hasn't this just cropped up?

The label implies (or should imply) that the hda is
common to both units but that they need different
electronic control modules to give them the
appropriate personality (a bit like the RA80/RM80/R80
from a short while back).

For some of these units I recall that the ecm is "matched"
to the drive as the last stage of manufacturing 
so that if the ecm fails you cannot just change
the ecm (even for one of the same type i.e. dssi to dssi).

I don't recall which units this true for.

I do believe that the RA73/RF73/RZ73 were the same HDA with
a different ECM.

Obviously spreading a single HDA design across multiple
busses would have made a great deal of sense and reduced
development costs considerbly.

I don't think that the RZ5x drives were done in this way
though, i.e. I think they were SCSI only.

> on us?  People seem to say they are incompatible. What gives?

People are right.

