It's not so bad...take the "business end" of a well insulated screwdriver
(the kind with the rubber handle) and slip it under that rubber cup on the
CRT, being careful not to scratch the glass. When you hear (and possibly
see) the large arc to ground (the outside of the tube,) the system is safe
to poke around (assuming it is unplugged, of course.)

If you still feel nervous doing this, put on a rubber glove too. I used to
service monochrome CRTs years ago, for dedicated PCs then called "word
processors." Once you've done it a couple of times, it's not so
nerve-racking.

The VT-100, like many terminals, has a feature that "turns off" the beam if
there is no horizontal (and maybe vertical) scanning detected. This prevents
the beam from burning a hole in the phosphor. There may be no high voltage
at all on your unit because of this. I have a VT-103 that has a similar
problem, but, like you, I have no schematics, and can't find anything
"obvious."

I was hoping to find a spare to try swapping modules (the video card, power
supply, logic board, etc,) just to try to isolate the problem, but so far no
luck. Do you have a friend with a VT-100 that would consent to having it be
a temporary "organ donor"?

