I started collecting information on this about 18 months ago.  Other 
circumstances have intervened, but now I REALLY am about to set up a VMS 
connection at home.  Since 18 months is an eternity in IT land, it's 
probably best to start from scratch.

What I want are a few static IP addresses and a dial-in as well as
dial-out on demand setup (or, if it's not much more expensive, a real 
permanent connection).  My machines should at least think they are 
permanently connected to the internet over IP, and from outside it 
should look like this as well.

18 months ago, the best solution seemed to be an ISDN router in 
conjunction with an ISDN line.  What about using television cable?  What 
about ADSL or whatever it's called?

I might have an extra Intel-PC sitting around at some point.  What are 
the pros and cons of using that as a router, running linux, as opposed 
to a "real" ISDN router?

Presumably, there has to be SOMETHING between the ISDN line or whatever 
and the VMS box (perhaps not in principle, but in practice).

I don't want to be tied to a specific provider, so the whole thing 
should be set up such that I could easily switch.  Presumably, IP 
numbers belong to the provider, but I guess I could switch these easily 
enough if the provider (or in principle someone else) put my machines, 
or at least my own DNS server, into his DNS records.  Certainly it would 
probably be better to register domain names etc independent of the 
provider.

Is there a FAQ for all this somewhere (if not for VMS at home, at least 
for the domain-name registration business)?  If necessary, take into 
account that this will be from Germany.


