This subject has been visited here from time to time.  There are several
devices on the market here in the US (and I would assume available out-
side the US too with appropriate mods for other countries' standards) to
allow you to connect to dial-up, ISDN, xDSL and cable with ease.  Many
of these same devices provide NAT/routing for several (4 and sometimes
more) machines sharing the single connection.  These devices offer both
DHCP servers as well as ways to disable the DHCP and use your own static
IP definitions.

3com offers their "OfficeConnect" line of products for dialup and ISDN.
They can be configured to dial in and stay connected, or to time out and 
dial on demand.

In the xDSL and cable realm, the DSL and cable modems typically provide
a 10base connection.  Thus, on your network side, you don't really know
the difference -- cable or xDSL.  Several companies have "routers" that
also provide NAT connection sharing.  Linksys, Ugate, and others permit
several devices (more if you uplink to a larger hub) to share this con-
nection.  Again, DCHP or static address configuration options.

There are limitations to NAT which may or may not be a hinderance to you.

The aforementioned solutions can be had in the US$100 to US$250 price
range.

On the high end, there are Cisco routers (or some similar router) which
can be connected to the appropriate interface (csu/dsu for example) and
then to various dedicated services.  Such services can usually get you
a set of IP addresses (usually subnetted from a larger set owned by the
provider) which you can then assign to your machines.  Prices on this
sort of server vary widely but, in general, are far pricier that any of
the dialup, ISDN, xDSL and cable options. 

You may be able to get a single static IP and a DNS advertisement for
it from a dialup, ISDN, xDSL or cable provider.  If you use the router
which provides NAT, you can have a machine which will be able to serve
as a webserver, SMTP server, FTP server, etc. and still connect inside
to all of your machines.  With the NAT router, if the connection to the
outside world is unavailable, your inside network is still available.
With NAT, you can also switch providers (which would likely mean you
would get a new IP address) or use a provider which leases addresses
via DHCP without having to reconfigure the machines and addressing
scheme you use internally.

I wanted to do a session on just this at the CETS symposium but I was
very dissatisfied with most of the vendors of these devices when I was
trying to get the session together.  Linksys, for example, after sit-
ting on the phone on hold for 3.5 hours (thankfully, it was their 800
number) I was asked what operating system I was using.  I said VMS. 
The person on the phone said, "that is not supported" and hund up.  Sub-
sequent calls and eMails to their support got me nowhere fast.  Thus,
I decided to NOT endorse, or make it appear that I would endorse, any of
these vendors.  Be forwarded!  If you go with any of these devices, the
vendors will be less then amicable unless you tell them you run Billy-
warez even though most of the issues you may have are totally unrelated
to the operating system of the devices you connect.

