In article <8s77mo$80m$1@bob.news.rcn.net>, "Carmine Castiglia" <ccastiglia@engsint.com> writes:
:Need to extend a LATNET network into a temporary warehouse (~ 1 year?) for a
:couple of ascii terminals and one or two queued, low-usage, printers.  Would
:like to use an 8 port 90L+ Terminal Server (have a couple unused sitting in
:a closet) at the warehouse end and would like to connect to main LATNET via
:POTS dial-up telephone line.

  LAT is not a routed network protocol.

  Either bridge or switch the LAN (if you have a sufficiently high bandwidth 
  link) and effectively extend LAT and other non-routed protocols out into 
  the target environment, or look at hanging some sort of mux off the terminal 
  server(s) and use the dial-up or similar link.  

  Other options include fractional T1, ASDL, IDSN or similar link between
  the sites, and/or use of a routed protocol such as IP over the link.

  Some DECserver boxes also support PPP or similar connections on the
  serial-line side, and IP connections on the network side.

  56 Kilobits per second (and only one way at a time) is the best you will 
  get on a dial-up circuit, and that's only ever going to happen downhill 
  with a strong tailwind -- further, most any serial printer can chew up a 
  sizeable chunk of that bandwidth.  (I've seen relatively few dialups ever 
  running faster than roughly 28Kb, too -- the top speed is very dependent 
  on the quality of the individual telephone circuits.)

 --------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
   Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




