> Need to extend a LATNET network into a temporary warehouse (~ 1 year?) for a
> couple of ascii terminals and one or two queued, low-usage, printers.  Would
> like to use an 8 port 90L+ Terminal Server (have a couple unused sitting in
> a closet) at the warehouse end and would like to connect to main LATNET via
> POTS dial-up telephone line.

We've used DEC Routeabout Access EW routers in bridging mode to run remote
LAT networks; it works fine with a 56K dedicated line, but I'd be leary
of trying it on a dialup.  Routeabouts do support dial-up WAN connections
(mainly for use as a backup link if a dedicated link fails) but we've
never tried that.  I don't know how well LAT would work with slower
links (since it is actual LAT protocol being bridged across the link).

The best solution we had for lower speed lines (but still dedicated) was
the MUXserver 3xx or MUXserver 90 series with DECmux 300 units.  I _think_
these support use as a dialup (though I'm pretty sure you need to use 
synchronous modems and have the proper adapter cables).  These worked
very well over 19,200 baud lines, and have the ability to set the
printer ports as "background" to minimize their impact on the interactive
terminals.  This equipment is long discontinued; you'd need to look for
a used equipment vendor.  Sorry, I cannot provide info on the cables or
specific modems.

