In article <suf452o2vl9f82@corp.supernews.com>, "John E. Malmberg" <wb8tyw@qsl.net> writes:
:"NetBSD Bob" <nbsdbob@weedcon1.cropsci.ncsu.edu> wrote
:in message news:8s7jpk$ffi$1@uni00nw.unity.ncsu.edu...
:> I am wanting to combine a TK70 as primary tape and a TK50 as
:> secondary tape in an old MVII.  What switch settings do I need
:> to set on the TQK50 card to do this, or is it just plug and play?
:
:It is definitely not plug and play!

  No bus this old is.  The BI bus was one of the earlier attempts to try
  to simplify the configuration and management of the bus hardware.  
  (Please note: I'm not really interested in discussing the non-technical 
  issues related to the BI bus.)

:Configuring Q-BUS address and CSR Vectors can be very challenging.

  Once you have some experience -- and you have the manuals for each of 
  the Q-bus modules, and access to a system for determining the needed
  CSR and vector settings.  And some grant cards.  (Thankfully, I only
  ever needed a wire-wrap tool for the UNIBUS.)  Oh, and you need to know
  the pattern for the serpentine layout for the particular Q-bus enclosure.
  And you also have to figure out which is "on" and which is "off" on each 
  switchpack -- the switchpack design, source and labeling could vary within
  a particular series of modules.  And once you know what a Q-bus hang or
  timeout looks like.  Yeah, once you understand that stuff, configuring 
  a Q-bus is easy.  :-)

  Seriously, a Q-bus system is not a good hobbyist system -- well, not
  for folks inexperienced with maintaining the Q-bus.

:The configuration program is also built into the console rom of most VAXen
:that are VAX 3500 or newer.  (I have heard of VAX 3500s that do not have
:this program though).

  A very few and very early KA650 CPU modules did not have the new console
  interface and tools -- these are usually easily identified, as the console
  on these looks and works like that of a MicroVAX II KA630.

:If you do not have access to an OpenVMS system, I would suggest posting on
:comp.os.vms a list of all of your devices that are on your Q-BUS, and
:probably some one would run the configuration program for you to give you
:the results.

  Emphasis on ALL Q-bus devices.  I repeat: ALL Q-bus devices.  I say 
  again: ALL -- do NOT omit any Q-bus devices from the Q-bus configuration 
  process, or the resulting Q-bus configuration settings can end up 
  non-functional.  If you don't know what the Q-bus widget is, then 
  (assuming it is a DIGITAL Q-bus board) post the M number from the 
  spine of the card and somebody here can look it up for you.

 --------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
   Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




