This would, I believe, be theoretically possible. On the other hand pursuing
such a course would reflect a unilateral action on the part of the Board of
Directors.

While significantly more than a majority of our members supported the bylaw
change referendum that took place last month , the vote did not meet the 2/3
requirement needed for the adoption of new bylaws. (The vote was 514 in
favor, 311 opposed). Further discussions with members and with participants
in a Bylaws Committee conference call revealed no significant opposition to
the incorporation of DECUS per se; the participants realized the
consequences of failing to move forward.

Invoking the Board's emergency powers to enact bylaws required for
incorporation enabled the Board to do what was necessary to contract with an
association management firm and to begin planning for Y2K01 activities
including CETS2001, international joint advocacy, and Chapter-specific
advocacy programs. Changes made under these circumstances require
ratification by the membership, and ballots should be in the mail right now.
Hence the membership will have the final say; which would not be the case
had the Board dissolved DECUS and formed Encompass, Inc.

The ballot that's enroute now will include a question relative to the
passage of incorporation-related bylaws as well as questions relative to the
passage of seven amendments. If the incorporation-related bylaws fail to
pass by the required two-thirds majority, the seven amendments are moot (as
is the concept of a viable user group). The seven amendments were drafted as
a result of the activities of the Bylaws Committee and relate to matters
including term limits, motions to recall Directors, means by which members
can be nominated for a Director election, electronic voting, etc. Please
read the ballot material carefully before casting your vote!






