Elmar, 

The memory used in many, but not all, recent DEC-made computers is
fairly straightforward SIMM or DIMM memory.  As a rule, if you can
find out the specifications, you can match the memory exactly with
off-the-shelf memory, Kingston, SMART, or generic.

Sounds like your board uses DIMMs, so the things to check are:

Memory operating voltage, usually 3.3v, older memory is 5v
Memory bus speed, 66Mhz, 83Mhz (somewhat unique to DEC) or 100Mhz
Buffered or unbuffered memory
If 100MHz memory, is it registered, i.e. does it have a serial
presence detect PAL on the DIMM to report memory speed to the
motherboard?
DIMM clocks.  I've seen 2 and 4 clock DIMMs.  I can't explain this
very well.

... Ben Myers
