>NetBSD Bob <nbsdbob@weedcon1.cropsci.ncsu.edu> wrote:
>: I have run across a BA-213(?) box marked VAX DECsystem 5400.
>: What is the beast, VAX or MIPS?  The thing has a cpu board
>: that seems about 5 slots wide with 3 bnc connectors on it,
>: and what appears ordinary scsi buses, plus a place for a standard
>: MMJ terminal.  My curiosity is getting the better of me, and
>: I keep thinking there has gotta be some use for the critter.
>

>DECsystem 5400 is the KN210 CPU board/IO board set which are Q-bus
>quadcards, with their own private memory bus across the front,  via
>the IDC50s. One of those is actually DSSI - the top one on the CPU board,
>IIRC.
>
>So, its a SPIM R3000A with DSSI, Ethernet and a few bits and pieces.
>If its got BNC stuff (especially three) then its most likely for a 
>framebuffer that does sync-on-green.

The DECsystem 5400, 5500 and 5800 all include some flavor of VAX
CPU to run the diagnostics for those systems.  The 5400 and 5500
were Q-bus based systems that supported a subset of the Q-bus
options; communications stuff, KDA50, etc.  The 5800 when into
the same box as some of the VAX 6000 systems and support some
XMI options (maybe some VAXBI options).

By having the VAX present, it wasn't necessary to write native
MIPS diagnostics.  The VAX was only used to run the diagnostics.
If you have a box with both VAX and DECsystem written on it, then
it was probably doctored by a previous owner or was some odd
upgrade that didn't replace all the labels.

>
>Just treat it as a big weird DECstation :)
>

I don't think any of the Q-bus graphics subsystems were ever
supported, but somebody with a source kit may have been able
to port the GPX driver.  I heard rumors of people trying to
make it work.


