Gareth Boulton (on VMS) <gareth_b@pacbell.net.vms> writes:
> Given that this is a rather broad list, and that the majority of the machines
> are PCs, can the AlphaStation use generic 70ns, parity SIMMs? Or does it
> definitely require proprietary memory? I can't find anything definite, either
> from DEC/Compaq, or anywhere else (at least, anywhere else that doesn't
> manufacture RAM!).

  It's very generic memory. The original memory that comes with the box is
33-bit (word parity) 70ns 72-pin FPM memory with tin leads. However, 36-bit
SIMMs (regular byte parity) work fine in there, and I've tested this box w/
768MB of memory (6 128MB Kingston SIMMs). The generic Kingston part number
is KTMxX36L-yT, where "x" is the size (8 is a 32MB part, and so forth) and
"y" is the speed. 70 works, but 60 may be cheaper (for some odd reason).

  Also consider MemoryX: http://www.memoryx.net/72pinsimm.html

