Version 3.0 of the Ersatz-11 PDP-11 emulator is now available.  This is
mainly a maintenance update, but it coincides with the official release of
the new Linux version of E11, which is why the major version # is being bumped.

The main changes are as follows:

- DF: emulation now includes DDC DMS-11X controller and SSDM 100 RAM disk

- SET CPU has more options, to allow precise emulation of early PDP-11 models

- LPT driver finds PCI printer interfaces (up to 8 LPTs total now)

- LPT driver includes "SET LPTn: DATAPRODUCTS" option which enables a
  software implementation of the Data Products line printer interface,
  requiring only a special cable (you can make your own, or buy a cable
  from Black Box for about $40 and use it with a Centronics gender changer
  and a regular PC printer cable) but no additional hardware -- FWIW, if
  you *want* to do it with hardware, D Bit will have RS232 to Data
  Products converter boxes available in a few weeks, cheaper than the
  Black Box thing and with less buffering, which can actually be a good
  thing in some cases

- Most devices' interrupt priorities are now settable, and the default
  values switch automatically to match whether the CPU being emulated is
  a Q-bus or Unibus model (previously, all priorities used Unibus values,
  which is fine with DEC software but broke some user-written drivers)

- SET THROTTLE command allows slowing down emulation, to avoid breaking
  software timing loops

- SET/SHOW commands for floppy drives (default drive type can be changed
  from the values retrieved from CMOS)

- INSTALL command passes command line to plug-in emulation module (full
  and Linux versions only)

- PC11 paper tape reader/punch is no longer permanent, can be created and
  destroyed with MOUNT/DISMOUNT commands like any other device

- bug fixes and documentation corrections as usual

The Demo version of V3.0 is available from:

	http://www.dbit.com/demo.html

There are no current plans to release a Demo version for Linux, but this
may change in the future.

John Wilson
D Bit


