> Same thing for dua0.

Sadly with the MicroVAX II (KA630) you don't get much
in the way of diagnostic tools - the later CPUs at
least have SHOW QBUS and SHOW DEVICE commands
which will tell you at least roughly what you have
in the box and whether at least its QBUS interface is
working (or trying to work).

I missed the start of the thread - have you already detailed
exactly what boards you have in there? They usually have
an Mxxxx number stamped on them somewhere visible (i.e.
without having to remove the board). Sometimes it is pressed
into the metal edge of the board (like on the M7606/M7608/M7609)
and sometimes it is clearly displayed on a plastic tag (like
most of the dual height cards e.g. M7555 for the RQDX3 disk
controller).

The order and exact placement of the boards can be critical.
If, in removing the graphics card, a "hole" has been left,
you will find things do not work. The ordering and postion
matters because the BA23 and BA123 differ in exactly how the
grant signals are wired down the bus. If you have a hole
then either you need a grant card (G727?) or you need to shuffle
cards around ... in a BA23 you can kiss goodbye to most of 
the skin on your knuckles :-)

> > VMS is a tight fit on and RD series drives, you might get
> > V7.2 on a RD54 but I doubt on a RD53. Best is 2*RD54,
> > once rfor system, one for data.
> 
> ???
> I guess you mean the type of harddisk installed?
> There are three maxtor harddisks, all full high.

RD54 and RD53 (and RD52 and RD51, just pray you don't have these!)
are all the same size. Assuming they are original disks that were
shipped with the machine by DEC, somewhere on them they should have an
RD
number. I don't have one in front of me to look at right now but
I can check a few tomorrow.

There are two cables that feed into the disk - ensuring that these
are not loose at either end is a good start. Same goes for the
cable into the RQDX3.

> > How much RAM do you have?
> 
> Ask my VAX ;-) I don't know. There are two cards in it that
> are next to the most right card, that look like they could
> be some kind of memory card.

If you had anything later than a MicroVAX II you could ask it
and it would tell you (SHOW MEMORY, I think).

The right most card (in a BA123 ... largish box you can sit on, 
it has wheels) or the topmost card (in a BA23 ... overgrown desktop
PC size) will be the CPU. That will be connected to the memory
card(s) via a ribbon cable. In a BA23 there can be at most
two memory modules, in a BA123 there can be four.

A MicroVAX II has 1MB on board, the boards could be 1MB 
(M7607 - Dual height), 2MB (M7608, half-populated, Quad), 
4MB (M7608, fully populated, Quad) or 8MB (M7609, Quad).
If you have 16MB on the memory cards, the 1MB on the CPU
disables itself ... the CPU cannot handle more than
16MB.


Antonio

