Dennis Grevenstein <dennis@pcde.inka.de> writes:
> 2 M7168
> 1 M7169

  These constitute an 8-plane color graphics processor. It should have a
bundle of coax cables that come out on a DB-15 on the back panel, as well
as some ribbon cables between the 3 cards.

  With this option installed, the console defaults to the graphics display,
which can be a pain if you don't have a keyboard/monitor. But if you hook
up a serial terminal to the usual console connector and hit BREAK a bunch
of times while it's powering up, you should get a console prompt (>>>) on
the serial terminal.

> 2 M7602

  An older bitonal monochrome adapter. These aren't all in one system,
are they?

> 1 M7546

  TK50 controller.

> 1 M 310? , last digit is covered.

  A serial controller (multiplexor). The model depends on the missing 4th
digit.

> What could this be?
> Is it possible to install some of these, e.g. a second
> CPU board?

  Urk. Yes, you can put some boards in, but swapping boards involves issues
of bus order and grant continuity, as well as setting jumpers/switches for
"floating CSR's and vectors".

  If the CPU has Q-bus edge connectors, you can only put one in a system.
All of the multiprocessor configs (except the unsupported MVII slave) have
non-Q-bus connectors and require a special chassis.

